docs/USER_MANUAL.md:237:The server also offers the tool `nexus_verify`: `nexus verify` for the client, on the working tree against the commit that was `HEAD` when the server started. The client passes no arguments. Only the verifier and deliverables you give at start are used (`nexus --authoritative-verifier "npm test" --deliverable-file src/parser.ts serve-mcp`); without `--authoritative-verifier` the tool runs no verifier and reports `BLOCKED` (or `UNVERIFIED` when the repository defines no verifier). The result carries a receipt id, and the receipt is read back at `nexus://verify-receipt/<id>` while the server runs. The ACP server offers `_nexus/verify` for an active session, using the verifier and deliverables fixed by the operator when Nexus starts; `_nexus/receipt/get` retrieves its receipt by id within that session. Verification is refused while a session is changing the same workspace. A client that can run commands as your user can still tamper outside the protocol (installed dependencies, your Git configuration); see [MCP_GUIDE.md](MCP_GUIDE.md).
docs/MCP_GUIDE.md:136:The ACP server exposes the same service as `_nexus/verify`. It accepts only an active `sessionId`; the verifier and deliverables are fixed by the operator configuration, and the base commit is pinned when the session starts or loads. Verification is refused while an overlapping session is prompting or stopping. Read the result with `_nexus/receipt/get` using the returned receipt id and the same session id. See [ACP.md](ACP.md) for the protocol surface.
